   "id": "spcpt_YmydrVnY8qSq7dcW",
   "name": "Properties of Quarks",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Properties of quarks"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The charge of a **hadron **is determined by the **sum **of the charges of its **quarks**\n- Each flavour of quark has a certain relative charge:"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Relative charge of quarks"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/11.2.2-Charge-of-quarks.png",
   "file": "assets/68c69b3da718-11-2-2-charge-of-quarks.png",
   "alt": "Charge of quarks, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Each flavour of quark has a charge of either +⅔e or -⅓e***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- For example, a proton is made up of two up quarks and a down quark. Adding up their charges gives the charge of a proton:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\mathrm{uud}=p$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=+1e$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The equivalent **antiparticle **of the quark is the **anti-quark**\n- These are identical to quarks except with opposite relative charges"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Anti-quarks"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/11.2.2-Charge-of-anti-quarks.png",
   "file": "assets/ff725b510a0f-11-2-2-charge-of-anti-quarks.png",
   "alt": "Charge of anti-quarks, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Each flavour of anti-quark has a charge of either -⅔e or +⅓e. The quark composition of anti-protons and anti-neutrons changes to anti-quarks***"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Particles are made up of a combination of three quarks or two quarks. Which quark combination would **not** give a particle a charge of -1 or 0?\n\n**A. **up, strange, strange\n\n**B.** charm, charm, down\n\n**C. **top, anti-up\n\n**D.** anti-up, anti-up, anti-strange\n\n**Answer: B**\n\n- Add up the charge of the individual quarks\n\n  - Charm, charm, down = $\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=+1$\n- The other quark combinations add up to either 0 or −1\n\n  - A: Up, strange, strange = $\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=0$\n  - C: Top, anti-up = $+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}=0$\n  - D: Anti-up, anti-up, anti-strange $-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=-1$\n  \n    - Remember that anti-particles have the opposite signs"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "You will be expected to remember the charge of each quark. However, instead of memorising the charges of anti-quarks too, just remember they are identical but with opposite signs."
  }
 ]
}